winSync CHANGELOG
=================

2026-10-28 - v1.3: SDR Console (QO-100) as a third, read-only radio source
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Added
  * SDR Console as a read-only source: [cat] source = "sdrconsole", a new [sdrconsole] section
    (port = "COM23", baud = 57600, url), --source sdrconsole, and "SDR Console" in the window's Radio
    selector. winSync/sdrconsole.py reads SDR Console's CAT serial port (Kenwood TS-2000 subset) over a
    com0com pair with pyserial (now in requirements.txt): NA; once for the radio name, then IF; about 10
    times a second for frequency, TX (IF[28]) and mode (IF[29]).
  * Only FA; IF; MD; NA; can ever be written (SafePort / check_command). In SDR Console TX; and RX; switch
    to transmit and receive; they are refused like everything else, and a test tries every other
    two-letter command, bare and with parameters.
  * Mute on TX from IF[28]. If SDR Console stops answering or the port goes during TX, winSync unmutes.
  * Clear messages when COM23 doesn't exist, is in use, or SDR Console doesn't answer (e.g. COM22 not
    ticked). COM23 is opened only while the source runs and closed when winSync stops, including when
    the window closes.
  * The page shows "SDR Console: connected (name)", "RX" instead of a VFO, and always says "SDR Console
    is read-only: tuning the receiver doesn't move the radio"; Control and Follow are greyed out.
  * With SDR Console the receiver defaults to the Goonhilly QO-100 narrowband WebSDR
    (https://eshail.batc.org.uk/nb/). Thetis and OmniRig still use [kiwi] url (M0CKE Kiwi).
  * tests/fake_sdrconsole.py (over TCP via pyserial socket://, or on a COM port), tests/test_sdrconsole.py,
    tests/test_page_sources.js, and server, window and launcher tests.
Changed
  * A receiver URL keeps its path (https://eshail.batc.org.uk/nb/): the whole site is still proxied at
    the root, and the page opens the receiver at its path. URLs at the root behave exactly as before.
    /api/target and /api/config also return "page" and "path"; "target" is still the bare origin.
  * bridge.js: on winSync's plain http page, sockets to "wss://" + winSync's own address become ws://
    (Goonhilly hard-codes wss:// for audio and waterfall); winSync connects upstream with wss. Other
    hosts, ws:// sockets and https pages are untouched.
  * Control forced to "radio" and /api/control refused now apply to every read-only source (OmniRig as
    before, same message).
  * winSync.cmd also checks for pyserial, so a v1.2 install runs the setup once by itself and gets it.
Verified, with the fake SDR Console only (no radio), 2026-10-28
  * The real Goonhilly page through winSync in headless Edge: /nb/ loads with the bridge, is detected as
    a WebSDR, becomes ready and was tuned to 10489.730 MHz USB from the fake; its /~~stream (audio) and
    /~~waterstream0 (waterfall) sockets were upgraded through winSync and stayed open with no upstream
    errors.
  * Upgrade: pyserial removed from a .venv; the new launcher check sent it to setup, which added
    pyserial 3.5 with no questions.
  * Not yet tested live with SDR Console and the radio.

2026-10-21 - v1.2: OmniRig as a read-only source for other radios
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Added
  * OmniRig as an alternative, read-only source for conventional radios: [cat] source = "omnirig",
    [omnirig] rig = 1 or 2, --source/--rig, and a Radio selector in the winSync window. winSync/omnirig.py
    reads only Status, StatusStr, RigType, ReadableParams, Freq, FreqA, FreqB, Vfo, Mode and Tx through
    COM (pywin32, now in requirements.txt) and never sets a property or calls a method (ReadOnlyRig,
    tests/test_omnirig.py with a fake OmniRig that records every access). Two-way sync is off with
    OmniRig: Control is fixed to Radio controls Kiwi.
  * The page always says "OmniRig is read-only: tuning the Kiwi doesn't move the radio".
  * The page shows "OmniRig: connected" and OmniRig's state (not installed, rig not responding, no TX
    reporting, unknown mode).
  * README: how to test OmniRig with Thetis as a TS-2000 over a com0com virtual COM port pair.
Fixed
  * With the winSync server stopped, the page's status box flickered every 2 s between "winSync
    server: reconnecting…" and the last radio state (Thetis too, since v1.0). It now says
    "reconnecting…" steadily until the server is back (tests/test_page_reconnect.js).
  * Stopping winSync from the window left OmniRig running and polling the radio: the server exits
    with os._exit, which skipped releasing OmniRig. It now releases it first (omnirig.release_all).
Verified, live with OmniRig 1.20 and Thetis v2.10.3.15 as a TS-2000 over com0com, 2026-10-21
  * The first run lost Rig 1 straight after connecting (RPC_E_DISCONNECTED): winSync released OmniRig's
    main object, and OmniRig then disconnects its Rig objects. Fixed (the object is kept while polling),
    with a test. After that: OmniRig Rig 1 online: TS-2000, 7.130000 MHz LSB RX, matching Thetis.
  * OmniRig 1.20 lists only COM1-COM20, so its end of the com0com pair must be COM20 or lower.
  * Following Thetis's VFO A through OmniRig: frequency, LSB/USB/CWU/AM, MOX and TUNE (both show TX).
    Tuning the Kiwi never moved the radio. Stopping winSync and closing the window both let OmniRig
    close by itself (about 2 s later). Then tested by the user before release.

2026-10-21 - v1.1: optional two-way sync (Kiwi -> radio)
--------------------------------------------------------
Added
  * Optional two-way sync: a Control selector on the page and in the winSync window (used when
    winSync starts), [sync] control in config.toml and --control: "radio" (default, unchanged from
    v1.0), "kiwi" (tuning the Kiwi sets the radio) or "both" (last tuned wins).
  * The radio is only changed by ZZFA/ZZFB (frequency) and ZZMD/ZZME (LSB, USB, CWL, CWU, FM, AM, SAM)
    of the followed VFO, while receiving (and 1 s after TX), inside the UK amateur bands as Thetis's UK
    region defines them, from a reading at most 0.5 s old. Anything else is refused by cat.py; a test
    tries all 421 Thetis CAT commands. Out of band, the page says "Radio not moved: ... outside the UK
    amateur bands (Kiwi only)".
  * MI0BOT "CAT to VFO B" is detected with the read ZZFA; and VFO A is then never set. A set that
    shows up on the other VFO stops control.
  * static/kiwiwatch.js (Kiwi change detection, with debounce and echo suppression),
    tests/test_control.py, tests/test_kiwiwatch.js and tests/page_e2e.js.
  * Runaway guard: more than 5 Kiwi -> radio changes within 6 s stops control until Control is
    chosen again. --dry-run and --diag for testing.
Verified, live against Thetis v2.10.3.15 (MI0BOT HL2) and the M0CKE Kiwi, 2026-10-21
  * Single sets by hand: ZZFA00007160000; moved VFO A 7.161 -> 7.160 and back, ZZMD01; / ZZMD00;
    switched RX1 LSB -> USB -> LSB; VFO B, mode and TX state untouched; no reply to an accepted set.
  * First run in Both: VFO A was stepped down 10 Hz about once a second (13 sets, 7.160990 ->
    7.160890 MHz, all in RX) without anyone tuning the Kiwi, until winSync was stopped by hand. Not
    reproduced since; a page race that could cause it was fixed and the runaway guard added.
  * Dry run with --diag: the Kiwi readback did not change at all in 3 minutes untouched; each
    typed frequency gave exactly one change.
  * Live in Both with --diag: 7150 and 7155 typed in the Kiwi gave exactly one set each; 7300 was
    refused as outside the UK bands (Kiwi only). With TUNE on, 7140 was refused ("the radio is
    transmitting"); after TUNE off, 7130 was set. With Control on Radio controls Kiwi nothing was
    sent. 3 sets in total, all as typed.

2026-10-19 - v1.0: Windows / Thetis version of piSync v0.9
----------------------------------------------------------
Added
  * winSync: piSync v0.9 (M0AWS) ported to Windows 10/11 and Thetis (v2.10.3.15, MI0BOT Hermes Lite 2
    build) in place of piHPSDR. Same design: read-only CAT, reverse proxy plus bridge.js to tune the
    KiwiSDR/WebSDR, mute on TX, tkinter control window.
  * winSync/cat.py talks to Thetis's TCP/IP CAT server (127.0.0.1:13013) and polls the five read commands
    ZZIF; ZZFB; ZZME; ZZRS; ZZXV; as one batch about 10 times a second. Nothing else is ever sent (enforced
    in code and by tests). The commands and reply formats were checked in Thetis's CATCommands.cs and
    against a running Thetis (results in README.md).
  * Follow: Thetis does not report a selected receiver, so the Kiwi follows VFO A while RX2 is off and,
    with RX2 on, the VFO tuned last. A Follow selector on the page (Auto / VFO A / VFO B), [sync] follow in
    config.toml and --follow on the command line override this.
  * TX (for Mute on TX) covers MOX, CAT PTT and TUNE.
  * Thetis mode numbers map to Kiwi modes, including SAM -> sam; [modes] overrides use Thetis numbers.
  * winSync.cmd: a double-click launcher. The first run (setup.ps1) finds or installs (winget, per user)
    Python 3.12, creates .venv, installs aiohttp and adds Desktop and Start menu shortcuts.
    --setup and --uninstall are also available.
  * tests/fake_thetis.py: fake Thetis CAT server for tests and manual runs.
  * winSync logo (winSync-Logo.png) and icons (assets/winsync-icon-*.png, assets/winsync.ico): piSync's
    artwork with "winSync" and "Thetis <-> WebSDR" lettering; icons of 64 px and below show only the
    tower and arrows. Rebuilt by tools/make_logo.py (needs Pillow).
Changed from piSync
  * piHPSDR-specific commands (AI2;, IF;, FR;, FB;, ZZME; with its ZZMD label quirk) are replaced by the
    Thetis ZZ commands above.
  * setup.sh, piSync.service and the Linux installer are replaced by winSync.cmd and setup.ps1.
  * The server runs without a console window and exits when the winSync window closes or crashes (its
    stdin is a pipe from the window).
  * The receiver list is saved in %APPDATA%\winSync\urls.json.
  * Pages are served under /_winsync/; the bridge object is window.winSync.
Removed
  * FLRig support (not needed with Thetis's own CAT server).
Verified
  * 30 automated tests pass (tests/, with the fake Thetis CAT server).
  * Live against Thetis v2.10.3.15 (MI0BOT HL2): state, the Follow selector and the Kiwi proxy work.
  * Mute on TX, tested live: CW in CWU sent from Thetis's CWX form shows TX in the log while keyed and RX
    afterwards, and the Kiwi was silent throughout. TUNE in LSB also mutes. The 2-3 s before the Kiwi's audio
    stops or resumes is the Kiwi's own audio latency, not winSync.
